<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/northern-pacific-freight-house/320-1910-mar-8-fh-fire-cropped.jpg" alt="Fire destroying Northern Pacific's east freight house on March 8, 1910">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Northern Pacific Railway's east freight house, filled with flammable materials, was destroyed by fire on March 8, 1910. Despite efforts by Chief {% person "dave-taylor" %}Dave Taylor{% endperson %} and his volunteer firefighters, insufficient water pressure prevented three hose lines from containing the blaze. Losses to the building and its contents were estimated at $30,000. Twelve adjacent boxcars also caught fire but were moved and extinguished. Firefighters saved the Bingenheimer Mercantile lumber shed on the neighboring property.</p>

<p>The Heart River nearly overflowed during the 1910 spring thaw, prompting Northern Pacific to construct its new freight house and passenger depot on West Main Street. The new freight depot opened at the west end of town in September 1910. Its offices occupied the east end, separated from the freight room by a firewall. An outside passage led to the basement and second-floor offices, which accommodated division headquarters.</p>

<p>Railroad officials boasted that the freight house was the largest and most elaborate building of its kind between the Twin Cities and the West Coast. Its immense size reflected Northern Pacific's expectation that Mandan would soon grow to between 15,000 and 20,000 residents.</p>

