<p>A telemarketing firm purchased and renovated the building in 1992, although Burlington Northern Santa Fe Railway continued to own the land. The operation lasted only two years. A local developer eventually bought the building, which then remained vacant for several years.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/northern-pacific-freight-house/356-cropped-mandan-library-west.jpg" alt="Former freight house converted into the Morton Mandan Public Library">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In 2003, BNSF offered the city a reduced land lease if Mandan purchased the former freight house for conversion into a library. Annual rent for the land beneath the freight house and the city-owned {% place "northern-pacific-colonial-depot" %}depot{% endplace %} and {% place "northern-pacific-beanery" %}Beanery{% endplace %} would fall from $6,080 to $500. In 2004, BNSF transferred the land to the city as part of a ground-oil cleanup settlement.</p>

